1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,753
In three days in September 2007, savers pulled four point

2
00:00:04,753 --> 00:00:09,114
four five billion pounds out of a single British bank.

3
00:00:09,783 --> 00:00:14,898
That was nearly a fifth of everything its retail customers had deposited.

4
00:00:14,898 --> 00:00:19,383
And it happened on television, with queues outside the branches.

5
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:23,608
MPs later called it the first run on the retail

6
00:00:23,608 --> 00:00:27,927
deposits of a United Kingdom bank since Victorian times.

7
00:00:28,616 --> 00:00:30,885
So why did Northern Rock collapse?

8
00:00:30,885 --> 00:00:34,889
Not, the courts would say, because it had run out of assets.

9
00:00:34,889 --> 00:00:36,957
Because it had run out of cash.

10
00:00:37,816 --> 00:00:40,790
Today we go through the paperwork: the Bank of

11
00:00:40,790 --> 00:00:45,251
England's statements, a Commons committee report, the regulator's own

12
00:00:45,251 --> 00:00:48,354
internal audit, and a string of court judgments.

13
00:00:49,083 --> 00:00:52,665
And later, the strangest number in the whole file: why

14
00:00:52,665 --> 00:00:56,047
a bank the stock market still valued at hundreds of

15
00:00:56,047 --> 00:00:59,894
millions of pounds was judged to be worth exactly nothing.

16
00:01:00,616 --> 00:01:01,336
Chapter 1.

17
00:01:01,336 --> 00:01:03,709
The bank that borrowed the world.

18
00:01:04,349 --> 00:01:10,093
Northern Rock was a former building society from the north-east of England.

19
00:01:10,093 --> 00:01:12,237
By the summer of 2007 it was

20
00:01:12,237 --> 00:01:16,220
the country's fifth-largest bank by mortgage assets.

21
00:01:16,883 --> 00:01:23,402
In June 1998 its total assets were seventeen point four billion pounds.

22
00:01:23,402 --> 00:01:29,555
By June 2007 they were one hundred and thirteen point five billion.

23
00:01:30,149 --> 00:01:33,120
That is an average of about twenty-three per

24
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:35,821
cent a year, compounded over nine years.

25
00:01:36,483 --> 00:01:37,842
Here's the problem.

26
00:01:37,842 --> 00:01:41,991
Over the same period, money from ordinary savers grew only

27
00:01:41,991 --> 00:01:46,139
from ten point four billion pounds to twenty-four billion.

28
00:01:46,816 --> 00:01:50,465
By the summer of 2007, retail deposits made up

29
00:01:50,465 --> 00:01:54,353
just twenty-three per cent of what the bank owed.

30
00:01:55,016 --> 00:01:59,136
The economist Hyun Song Shin set out, from the bank's own

31
00:01:59,136 --> 00:02:04,050
accounts, that in June 2007 Northern Rock had forty-five point seven

32
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:09,687
billion pounds of securitised notes, another twenty-six point seven billion of

33
00:02:09,687 --> 00:02:14,746
other wholesale funding, and eight point one billion of covered bonds.

34
00:02:15,549 --> 00:02:19,655
The securitised notes came through a vehicle called Granite.

35
00:02:19,655 --> 00:02:23,419
Northern Rock bundled mortgages together, and investors

36
00:02:23,419 --> 00:02:26,020
bought bonds backed by the repayments.

37
00:02:26,683 --> 00:02:30,232
Reuters later reported that the strategy relied on

38
00:02:30,232 --> 00:02:34,420
wholesale markets for three-quarters of the bank's funding.

39
00:02:35,083 --> 00:02:39,384
The model worked as long as one thing stayed true: that investors

40
00:02:39,384 --> 00:02:43,486
would always be there to lend again when old funding came due.

41
00:02:44,149 --> 00:02:44,882
Chapter 2.

42
00:02:44,882 --> 00:02:46,859
The summer the market shut.

43
00:02:47,516 --> 00:02:52,283
On the twenty-seventh of June 2007, Northern Rock warned that its

44
00:02:52,283 --> 00:02:56,829
profits would fall short, because its funding costs had risen.

45
00:02:57,516 --> 00:03:01,158
Then, in August, the markets it depended on seized up.

46
00:03:01,158 --> 00:03:04,666
Shin dates the bank's demise to the ninth of August,

47
00:03:04,666 --> 00:03:08,713
when short-term funding and interbank lending all but froze.

48
00:03:09,383 --> 00:03:13,051
According to facts agreed before the European Court of

49
00:03:13,051 --> 00:03:17,399
Human Rights, Northern Rock alerted its regulator, the Financial

50
00:03:17,399 --> 00:03:20,660
Services Authority, on the thirteenth of August.

51
00:03:21,383 --> 00:03:26,535
By the third of September, the three authorities, the Treasury, the Bank of

52
00:03:26,535 --> 00:03:31,618
England and the FSA, had agreed in principle to provide emergency support.

53
00:03:32,283 --> 00:03:35,030
In the week of the tenth of September, Northern

54
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:37,835
Rock approached the Bank of England for support.

55
00:03:38,483 --> 00:03:41,993
The official statement came on Friday the fourteenth.

56
00:03:41,993 --> 00:03:45,569
The Chancellor had authorised the Bank to lend against

57
00:03:45,569 --> 00:03:49,211
appropriate collateral and at an interest rate premium.

58
00:03:49,883 --> 00:03:52,488
And the statement tried to reassure.

59
00:03:52,488 --> 00:03:56,613
The FSA judged that Northern Rock is solvent, exceeds its

60
00:03:56,613 --> 00:04:01,245
regulatory capital requirement and has a good quality loan book.

61
00:04:01,916 --> 00:04:05,340
It also named the weak spot plainly: the mortgage

62
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:10,230
securitisation market, on which Northern Rock is particularly reliant.

63
00:04:10,916 --> 00:04:13,085
But the news had already leaked.

64
00:04:13,085 --> 00:04:16,068
On the thirteenth, the BBC broadcast news of

65
00:04:16,068 --> 00:04:20,406
the bank's liquidity problems, before the official announcement.

66
00:04:21,083 --> 00:04:22,058
Chapter 3.

67
00:04:22,058 --> 00:04:23,131
The queues.

68
00:04:23,783 --> 00:04:28,103
What followed was a scene Britain had not seen in living memory.

69
00:04:28,103 --> 00:04:32,017
Customers queued outside branches to take their money out.

70
00:04:32,616 --> 00:04:36,358
Part of the reason was the deposit protection of the time.

71
00:04:36,358 --> 00:04:39,777
It covered the first two thousand pounds in full, but

72
00:04:39,777 --> 00:04:43,325
only ninety per cent of the next thirty-three thousand.

73
00:04:43,983 --> 00:04:47,610
So a saver with thirty thousand pounds stood to lose up

74
00:04:47,610 --> 00:04:51,304
to two thousand eight hundred pounds if the bank failed.

75
00:04:51,304 --> 00:04:54,008
Rushing to the branch was not irrational.

76
00:04:54,683 --> 00:04:59,897
Over three days, depositors withdrew four point four five billion pounds.

77
00:04:59,897 --> 00:05:04,539
A Yale study puts the outflow at at least four point six billion.

78
00:05:05,149 --> 00:05:06,797
The shares collapsed too.

79
00:05:06,797 --> 00:05:10,882
On the fourteenth, the Associated Press reported, they fell by

80
00:05:10,882 --> 00:05:14,769
thirty-one per cent to four hundred and thirty-eight pence.

81
00:05:15,449 --> 00:05:19,648
Shin argued it was not quite the classic run of the textbooks.

82
00:05:19,648 --> 00:05:22,290
In his account, the true run was in the

83
00:05:22,290 --> 00:05:25,947
wholesale markets, as maturing loans were not renewed.

84
00:05:25,947 --> 00:05:28,182
The queues were the visible part.

85
00:05:28,849 --> 00:05:32,608
On Monday the seventeenth, the Chancellor announced that

86
00:05:32,608 --> 00:05:36,165
the government would guarantee all existing deposits.

87
00:05:36,165 --> 00:05:37,508
The queues subsided.

88
00:05:38,183 --> 00:05:42,633
The guarantee was widened to new retail deposits on the ninth

89
00:05:42,633 --> 00:05:47,155
of October, and to a broader range of liabilities in December.

90
00:05:47,816 --> 00:05:52,258
And from the first of October, the national scheme itself changed.

91
00:05:52,258 --> 00:05:56,968
Deposits were now protected in full up to thirty-five thousand pounds.

92
00:05:57,583 --> 00:05:58,436
Chapter 4.

93
00:05:58,436 --> 00:06:00,313
The taxpayer steps in.

94
00:06:00,949 --> 00:06:03,632
The emergency loan did not stay small.

95
00:06:03,632 --> 00:06:08,008
By the ninth of October the first facility was nearly used up,

96
00:06:08,008 --> 00:06:12,314
and a second, secured on all the company's assets, was added.

97
00:06:12,983 --> 00:06:16,569
By the end of 2007, the Court of Appeal recorded,

98
00:06:16,569 --> 00:06:21,254
the Bank of England had lent almost twenty-seven billion pounds,

99
00:06:21,254 --> 00:06:25,572
with Treasury guarantees of about twenty-nine billion more.

100
00:06:26,316 --> 00:06:29,732
The government spent the winter looking for a buyer.

101
00:06:29,732 --> 00:06:33,936
On the seventeenth of February 2008, the Treasury announced that

102
00:06:33,936 --> 00:06:37,024
the private bids did not offer value for money.

103
00:06:37,683 --> 00:06:41,083
Northern Rock would be taken into temporary public

104
00:06:41,083 --> 00:06:44,484
ownership, with Ron Sandler as executive chairman.

105
00:06:45,149 --> 00:06:48,764
Parliament passed an emergency law, the Banking Special

106
00:06:48,764 --> 00:06:52,115
Provisions Act, dated the twenty-first of February.

107
00:06:52,115 --> 00:06:56,518
The next day the shares were transferred to the Treasury Solicitor.

108
00:06:57,183 --> 00:07:02,436
On that day, a tribunal later recorded, Northern Rock owed the Bank of England

109
00:07:02,436 --> 00:07:07,825
twenty-five point two eight billion pounds, almost a quarter of its book assets.

110
00:07:08,483 --> 00:07:12,488
The Court of Appeal summed up the paradox in two sentences.

111
00:07:12,488 --> 00:07:15,068
It was solvent in balance sheet terms.

112
00:07:15,068 --> 00:07:19,277
But it was unable to meet its debts as and when they fell due.

113
00:07:19,883 --> 00:07:23,855
The National Audit Office reviewed the decision in 2009.

114
00:07:23,855 --> 00:07:28,536
It found nationalisation was the best option for the taxpayer, but

115
00:07:28,536 --> 00:07:33,359
said the Treasury could have assessed the risks more systematically.

116
00:07:34,016 --> 00:07:39,845
The NAO also noted that, while on state support, the bank kept offering mortgages

117
00:07:39,845 --> 00:07:44,739
of up to one hundred and twenty-five per cent of a property's value.

118
00:07:45,416 --> 00:07:46,209
Chapter 5.

119
00:07:46,209 --> 00:07:47,635
Who was watching?.

120
00:07:48,283 --> 00:07:50,513
So where was the regulator?

121
00:07:50,513 --> 00:07:55,220
In March 2008, the Financial Services Authority published

122
00:07:55,220 --> 00:07:58,440
the findings of its own internal audit.

123
00:07:59,116 --> 00:08:03,556
It found four failings in the period leading up to the crisis.

124
00:08:03,556 --> 00:08:08,353
One was inadequate specific resource directly supervising the firm.

125
00:08:08,949 --> 00:08:13,980
Reporting on the audit, the finance magazine The CFO set out the detail.

126
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:18,101
There were no formal records of key meetings with the bank.

127
00:08:18,716 --> 00:08:23,473
And between January 2005 and August 2007, three different

128
00:08:23,473 --> 00:08:29,065
heads of department were responsible for supervising Northern Rock.

129
00:08:29,749 --> 00:08:34,297
Compared with the other firms it reviewed, the audit put Northern Rock's

130
00:08:34,297 --> 00:08:38,402
supervision at the extreme end of the spectrum on these failings.

131
00:08:38,402 --> 00:08:42,445
The FSA's chief executive, Hector Sants, said it was not carried

132
00:08:42,445 --> 00:08:46,108
out to a standard that is acceptable, though he added that

133
00:08:46,108 --> 00:08:50,403
whether that would have changed the outcome was impossible to judge.

134
00:08:51,183 --> 00:08:52,506
MPs went further.

135
00:08:52,506 --> 00:08:56,008
The Treasury Committee concluded that the FSA

136
00:08:56,008 --> 00:08:59,510
systematically failed in its regulatory duty.

137
00:09:00,183 --> 00:09:04,053
But the Committee placed the first blame on the bank itself.

138
00:09:04,053 --> 00:09:08,503
It said the directors were the principal authors of the difficulties.

139
00:09:09,116 --> 00:09:12,164
And it described their strategy as a reckless

140
00:09:12,164 --> 00:09:16,024
business model, excessively reliant on wholesale funding.

141
00:09:16,683 --> 00:09:20,818
Then, in 2010, the regulator found something else.

142
00:09:20,818 --> 00:09:24,043
Not about funding, but about bad loans.

143
00:09:24,783 --> 00:09:30,053
According to the FSA's final notice, the deputy chief executive, David

144
00:09:30,053 --> 00:09:34,948
Baker, knew from December 2006 that one thousand nine hundred and

145
00:09:34,948 --> 00:09:39,992
seventeen loans were missing from the bank's impaired-loan figures.

146
00:09:40,749 --> 00:09:45,659
Counting them would have lifted reported arrears from zero point four two

147
00:09:45,659 --> 00:09:49,896
per cent to zero point six eight per cent, and possessions from

148
00:09:49,896 --> 00:09:54,738
six hundred and sixty-two to two thousand five hundred and seventy-nine.

149
00:09:55,483 --> 00:10:00,719
The FSA found that Mr Baker's conduct demonstrated a lack of integrity.

150
00:10:00,719 --> 00:10:06,619
He was fined five hundred and four thousand pounds and banned from the industry.

151
00:10:07,216 --> 00:10:11,689
The managing credit director, Richard Barclay, was fined one hundred

152
00:10:11,689 --> 00:10:15,569
and forty thousand pounds and barred from senior roles, for

153
00:10:15,569 --> 00:10:19,516
failures of skill, care and diligence rather than integrity.

154
00:10:19,516 --> 00:10:24,054
The finance director, David Jones, was fined three hundred and twenty

155
00:10:24,054 --> 00:10:28,395
thousand pounds and banned; the FSA found he too lacked integrity.

156
00:10:29,183 --> 00:10:34,558
All three settled with the FSA, and their fines include settlement discounts.

157
00:10:34,558 --> 00:10:38,537
These were regulatory findings, set out in final notices.

158
00:10:38,537 --> 00:10:40,980
They were not criminal convictions.

159
00:10:41,583 --> 00:10:42,507
Chapter 6.

160
00:10:42,507 --> 00:10:43,801
Worth nothing.

161
00:10:44,449 --> 00:10:46,942
Now, the strangest number in the file.

162
00:10:46,942 --> 00:10:50,287
Immediately before nationalisation, Northern Rock's

163
00:10:50,287 --> 00:10:52,189
shares stood at ninety pence.

164
00:10:52,189 --> 00:10:54,485
The market valued the bank at about

165
00:10:54,485 --> 00:10:57,502
three hundred and seventy-nine million pounds.

166
00:10:58,249 --> 00:11:00,603
Shareholders expected compensation.

167
00:11:00,603 --> 00:11:04,705
But the emergency law set the rules for valuing their shares.

168
00:11:04,705 --> 00:11:08,134
The valuer had to assume that all state support had

169
00:11:08,134 --> 00:11:11,900
been withdrawn, and that the bank was in administration.

170
00:11:12,583 --> 00:11:16,761
Shareholders, including hedge funds, challenged the law.

171
00:11:16,761 --> 00:11:22,133
The largest shareholder, SRM, held more than forty-eight million shares.

172
00:11:22,133 --> 00:11:26,385
In July 2009 the Court of Appeal dismissed the challenge.

173
00:11:26,983 --> 00:11:33,313
The independent valuer, Andrew Caldwell, was appointed in September 2008.

174
00:11:33,313 --> 00:11:37,735
In March 2010, he assessed the compensation at nil.

175
00:11:38,349 --> 00:11:43,481
More than four hundred and forty former shareholders appealed.

176
00:11:43,481 --> 00:11:48,696
In October 2011 the Upper Tribunal dismissed every application.

177
00:11:49,283 --> 00:11:51,960
In 2012, the European Court of Human

178
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:55,828
Rights declared the shareholders' case inadmissible.

179
00:11:55,828 --> 00:11:58,877
It said the government's approach was far

180
00:11:58,877 --> 00:12:02,745
from being manifestly without reasonable foundation.

181
00:12:03,483 --> 00:12:07,773
One last appeal reached the Court of Appeal in 2013.

182
00:12:07,773 --> 00:12:13,467
It failed by two judges to one, with Lord Justice Lewison dissenting.

183
00:12:14,083 --> 00:12:18,408
On the valuer's assumptions, the court noted, the bank's surplus

184
00:12:18,408 --> 00:12:22,193
became a deficit of about two point four billion pounds.

185
00:12:22,193 --> 00:12:25,302
On those assumptions the valuer found, and the

186
00:12:25,302 --> 00:12:28,817
tribunal upheld, that the shares were worth nothing.

187
00:12:29,549 --> 00:12:30,588
Chapter 7.

188
00:12:30,588 --> 00:12:31,939
Our analysis.

189
00:12:32,583 --> 00:12:34,695
So what was the final bill?

190
00:12:34,695 --> 00:12:38,137
We can now answer it from the audit reports.

191
00:12:38,137 --> 00:12:43,143
In 2008 alone, Northern Rock lost one point four billion pounds.

192
00:12:43,749 --> 00:12:46,067
In 2010 it was split in two.

193
00:12:46,067 --> 00:12:50,121
A good bank kept the deposits and some mortgages.

194
00:12:50,121 --> 00:12:54,011
The rest stayed in public hands to be run down.

195
00:12:54,616 --> 00:13:00,061
In November 2011, Virgin Money agreed to buy the good bank for seven

196
00:13:00,061 --> 00:13:06,867
hundred and forty-seven million pounds in cash, potentially rising above one billion.

197
00:13:07,549 --> 00:13:12,291
The National Audit Office put the taxpayer's loss on that part at four

198
00:13:12,291 --> 00:13:17,914
hundred and eighty million pounds, against an investment of one point four billion.

199
00:13:18,583 --> 00:13:21,362
Across the whole rescue, the NAO counted

200
00:13:21,362 --> 00:13:24,419
thirty-seven billion pounds of cash support.

201
00:13:24,419 --> 00:13:29,421
UK Financial Investments, which managed the state's stake, expected that

202
00:13:29,421 --> 00:13:32,687
cash to be repaid, but the NAO said there could

203
00:13:32,687 --> 00:13:36,230
be a net present cost of around two billion pounds.

204
00:13:37,049 --> 00:13:42,104
In May 2016, a Cerberus-led consortium completed its purchase of

205
00:13:42,104 --> 00:13:47,632
thirteen point three billion pounds of former Northern Rock mortgages.

206
00:13:47,632 --> 00:13:53,397
The NAO found the price was seventy-four million pounds above face value.

207
00:13:54,083 --> 00:13:56,305
Here is our reading of the record.

208
00:13:56,305 --> 00:14:00,488
Northern Rock did not fail because its borrowers stopped paying.

209
00:14:00,488 --> 00:14:03,691
It failed because it borrowed short to lend long,

210
00:14:03,691 --> 00:14:06,305
on a scale its savers could never cover.

211
00:14:06,983 --> 00:14:11,513
The regulator, by its own audit, was not watching closely enough.

212
00:14:11,513 --> 00:14:13,883
And inside the bank, the FSA later

213
00:14:13,883 --> 00:14:17,089
found, the arrears figures had been flattered.

214
00:14:17,749 --> 00:14:21,547
The shareholder cases are the part most explainers skip.

215
00:14:21,547 --> 00:14:26,225
The courts accepted a simple idea: value created by a taxpayer rescue

216
00:14:26,225 --> 00:14:30,226
does not belong to the people who owned the bank before it.

217
00:14:30,883 --> 00:14:33,506
Whether that was fair is still argued.

218
00:14:33,506 --> 00:14:35,992
One Court of Appeal judge disagreed.

219
00:14:35,992 --> 00:14:40,824
But the shareholders lost in every court and tribunal that heard them.

220
00:14:41,416 --> 00:14:43,746
And the queues did change the rules.

221
00:14:43,746 --> 00:14:46,789
Full protection of deposits, rather than ninety

222
00:14:46,789 --> 00:14:48,925
per cent, dates from that autumn.

223
00:14:49,583 --> 00:14:53,717
Northern Rock was solvent on paper and broke in practice.

224
00:14:53,717 --> 00:14:56,111
That gap is where bank runs live.

225
00:14:56,716 --> 00:15:00,566
Every source for this episode is numbered in the description.

226
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:04,353
If we've got something wrong, tell us, and we'll correct it.

227
00:15:04,949 --> 00:15:08,075
The next case file is Patisserie Valerie, the

228
00:15:08,075 --> 00:15:10,924
cafe chain whose accounts did not add up.

229
00:15:10,924 --> 00:15:13,285
Subscribe so it's waiting for you.
